WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.852
Processing can amplify both complexity and

2
00:00:02.852 --> 00:00:05.791
resource use far beyond the upload
request.

3
00:00:05.791 --> 00:00:08.829
Image decoders, document parsers, archive
extractors, media

4
00:00:08.829 --> 00:00:11.867
converters, metadata tools, scanners, and
indexing services

5
00:00:11.867 --> 00:00:14.807
all interpret structures chosen by the
sender.

6
00:00:14.807 --> 00:00:19.314
A small compressed file can describe a
large amount of output.

7
00:00:19.314 --> 00:00:24.214
An image's encoded byte size does not
bound its decoded pixel count.

8
00:00:24.214 --> 00:00:28.721
A request limit therefore protects a
different stage from processing limits.

9
00:00:28.721 --> 00:00:31.955
Set budgets for the actual work:
dimensions and

10
00:00:31.955 --> 00:00:35.483
pixels, document pages, archive entries,
nesting depth, decompressed

11
00:00:35.483 --> 00:00:39.206
bytes, output size, processing time, CPU,
and memory.

12
00:00:39.206 --> 00:00:41.656
Enforce limits while work proceeds, not

13
00:00:41.656 --> 00:00:44.596
only after all output has been allocated.

14
00:00:44.596 --> 00:00:47.928
Bound concurrency and temporary disk usage
as well.

15
00:00:47.928 --> 00:00:51.553
When a processor times out, stop its work
and

16
00:00:51.553 --> 00:00:54.787
remove partial output; returning an error
while the

17
00:00:54.787 --> 00:00:58.511
process continues consuming resources does
not enforce the budget.

18
00:00:58.511 --> 00:01:01.450
Archive extraction also crosses a
filesystem boundary.

19
00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:05.174
Every entry must resolve inside the
intended extraction root.

20
00:01:05.174 --> 00:01:09.583
Entry names do not get to choose arbitrary
destinations, and links

21
00:01:09.583 --> 00:01:14.091
or existing directory structures must not
redirect writes outside that root.

22
00:01:14.091 --> 00:01:17.717
Prefer a maintained extractor with safe
defaults, reject unsupported

23
00:01:17.717 --> 00:01:21.538
entry types, and extract into a fresh
restricted workspace.

24
00:01:21.538 --> 00:01:25.262
Verify containment for each output and
constrain total expansion.

25
00:01:25.262 --> 00:01:27.614
Nested archives require an explicit depth

26
00:01:27.614 --> 00:01:30.162
policy rather than unrestricted recursive
unpacking.

27
00:01:30.162 --> 00:01:32.513
Keep file processing libraries patched
because

28
00:01:32.513 --> 00:01:35.453
validation intentionally exposes parsers
to hostile bytes.

29
00:01:35.453 --> 00:01:38.393
Separate workers, sandboxed processes, or
restricted containers

30
00:01:38.393 --> 00:01:41.332
can limit the consequences of parser
failure.

31
00:01:41.332 --> 00:01:43.782
Use low privilege identities, restrict
filesystem

32
00:01:43.782 --> 00:01:46.624
access, and disable unnecessary network
access to

33
00:01:46.624 --> 00:01:49.172
prevent processors from reaching unrelated
resources.

34
00:01:49.172 --> 00:01:52.405
Isolation supplements format validation
and resource budgets; it

35
00:01:52.405 --> 00:01:55.737
does not decide what the feature should
accept.

36
00:01:55.737 --> 00:01:58.873
Treat derived outputs as new artifacts
requiring an

37
00:01:58.873 --> 00:02:02.009
expected format, bounded size, and
controlled promotion.

38
00:02:02.009 --> 00:02:04.360
Successful process exit alone should not

39
00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:07.300
cause every generated file to become
downloadable.

